Why Each New Component Was Added

1. Firewalls (3 Total)
- Firewall #1: Protects the public entrypoint before reaching the load balancer.
- Firewall #2: Protects the web/application servers from unwanted traffic.
- Firewall #3: Protects the database layer by restricting traffic to web/app servers only.
Why added:
Firewalls prevent unauthorized access, block malicious requests, and filter traffic based on rules.

2. SSL Certificate
The SSL certificate added to the load balancer allows HTTPS.
Why added:
- Encrypts data between users and the load balancer.
- Prevents eavesdropping and MITM attacks.
- Ensures authenticity and trust for www.foobar.com.

3. Monitoring Clients (3 Clients)
One monitoring agent is installed on:
- The load balancer
- Server 1
- Server 2
Why added:
Monitoring agents collect metrics (CPU, RAM, QPS, logs, events) and
 send data to a central monitoring service like SumoLogic, Datadog, or Prometheus.


Firewalls -  What They Are For

Firewalls filter network traffic.  
They:
- Allow only permitted ports/services.
- Block unauthorized access.
- Protect infrastructure from attacks such as port scans, brute force, and injection attempts.


Why Traffic Is Served Over HTTPS

- HTTPS encrypts all communication between the user and the server.
- Prevents data theft or interception.
- Protects passwords, cookies, and personal data.
- Confirms the identity of the website (authentication).

Serving a website without HTTPS is insecure because anyone on the network can read or tamper with data.


Monitoring -  Purpose and Data Collection

Monitoring is used to:
- Track system health (CPU, memory, disk, network).
- Track service health (web server uptime, database latency).
- Detect anomalies and failures.
- Alert administrators automatically.

How Monitoring Tools Collect Data:
- A monitoring agent runs on each server.
- It collects logs, metrics, and system data at intervals.
- Data is sent to a central monitoring platform (Sumologic, Datadog, Prometheus, etc.).
- Dashboards and alerts are configured from this data.

How to Monitor Web Server QPS (Queries Per Second):
- Configure the monitoring tool to scrape or read Nginx access logs.
- Count how many requests hit the server per second.
- Export the metric as ârequests_per_secondâ or QPS.
- Create charts and alerts when QPS exceeds thresholds.


Issues With This Infrastructure

1. SSL Termination at the Load Balancer
Issue:
- Traffic is decrypted at the load balancer, then forwarded to servers in plain HTTP.
Why this is bad:
- Traffic inside the network is no longer encrypted.
- If the internal network is compromised, attackers can read the data.
- Better practice is âend-to-end encryption  where back-end servers also use HTTPS.

2. Only One MySQL Server Accepting Writes
Issue:
- The Primary is the only writable database.
- If it fails, the entire system cannot process writes.
- The Replica cannot immediately take over unless failover is configured.
- This creates a Single Point of Failure (SPOF).

3. All Servers Have the Same Components
Issue:
- Both servers run the same: web server, application server, and database.
Problems:
- Complexity: each server must maintain its own identical stack.
- Synchronization: application files must be duplicated, increasing the risk of inconsistency.
- Security: more exposed services increase attack surface.
- Scaling: tying all components together makes scaling individual layers impossible.


